"""Concept lessons in syllabus order. The ¶ marker separates teaching paragraphs."""
from teaching.foundations import put

put(9, '''A chat history, a payment ledger, and a thumbnail cache all store data, but losing a copy has different consequences. Before choosing technology, state what is accepted, when it is committed, how callers read it, and which failures it must survive. ¶ Identify the authoritative record and the recovery procedure. A database category does not establish its configured isolation, durability, or backup behaviour. Begin with access patterns and invariants, then compare implementations against them. This prevents a familiar product name from standing in for decisions about the actual service being built. || Identify authoritative records / Define commit and read contract / State tolerated failures / Test recovery and retention

A ledger can represent accounts and entries as related tables. Keys identify rows, foreign keys protect appropriate relationships, and transactions can group changes. SQL expresses operations on this relational model. ¶ Joins let queries combine related facts. Their cost depends on indexes, row counts, data distribution, and the chosen execution plan; a join is not inherently a scaling mistake. Normalised facts often provide a clear starting point for ownership and consistency. If a measured read path needs a repeated or precomputed representation, add it deliberately and state how it stays correct. The relational model and the physical query cost are related but different questions. || Accounts keyed by ID / Entries reference accounts / Transaction protects related changes / Query plan determines access cost

A user profile and its preferences can be stored as one nested document. A document database gives that unit an identity and often allows flexible structures. This can suit data usually read or changed together. ¶ Decide which structure is valid and how it evolves. Flexibility moves some checking into application conventions; it does not remove the schema. Also ask how large the document can grow and whether changes across documents need a shared transaction. Placing every chat message in one ever-growing user document mixes different growth and ownership needs. Design the document boundary around actual operations rather than convenient JSON syntax. || Profile document owns preferences / Validate supported document version / Bound nested collection growth / Cross-document rules need explicit guarantees

A session lookup already knows its identifier. A key-value store can retrieve the corresponding value directly, making this a natural access pattern. A key such as tenant:42:session:7 also makes the intended identity scope visible. ¶ Other queries, such as finding values by an arbitrary field, may need separately maintained structures. Check the selected store's read, write, expiry, and durability contract; key-value products do not all behave alike. A session's expiry and revocation policy must also be explicit. Retaining an old value after permissions change can be a security problem even when the key lookup itself is correct. || Tenant-scoped key / Direct value lookup / Expiration or replacement / Secondary query needs another access path

A conversation history is often read by conversation identifier and message order. A wide-column design can use the conversation as a partition key and a sequence as an ordering dimension, supporting bounded history reads. ¶ Work through the largest room as well as the typical one. One huge or popular partition can dominate traffic despite many storage nodes. Model the queries first, and duplicate representations for different queries only with a maintenance plan. State which joins or integrity rules the application must enforce. The common storage pattern is useful guidance; exact guarantees still depend on the implementation. || Partition: conversation ID / Cluster by message sequence / Read bounded sequence range / Hot conversation remains one pressure point

A fraud investigator may follow transfers from an account to devices, other accounts, and further transfers. When variable-depth relationships are central to the query, graph storage can make traversals convenient. ¶ A relational model can also represent edges, so compare actual operations rather than assuming relationships require a graph database. Bound traversal depth and fan-out: one high-degree node can create enormous work. Apply access checks during expansion, not only after collecting results. A traversal that crosses tenant boundaries can expose data even if its starting node was authorised. Query usefulness, cost, and permission rules must be designed together. || Start at account / Traverse permitted device edges / Bound depth and fan-out / Return evidence with authorization scope

A temperature measurement has a time, value, and dimensions such as device identity. Time-series storage can exploit nearby timestamps, compression, and time-based retention for these measurements. ¶ Choose dimensions carefully. A unique request identifier on every metric can create too many distinct series. Also decide how to handle late readings, clock errors, corrections, and downsampling. A device timestamp records what its clock reported; it does not automatically establish causal order. Preserve ingestion time or a sequence where investigation needs that distinction. Time-based organisation helps when it matches the measurements and queries, rather than simply because a row contains a date. || Device measurement timestamp / Ingest with source identity / Partition and retain by time / Downsample without hiding required detail

A search query needs matching and ranking rather than a transaction over the original record. An inverted index maps terms to document references and supports this retrieval path. It is commonly built from another authoritative store. ¶ If indexing is asynchronous, a new document may appear late and deleted content may remain in the index temporarily. Permission enforcement must remain correct during that lag. Keep source identity and version information so updates can be ordered and the index rebuilt. A search result is a derived serving representation; treating it as the only recoverable copy creates different and usually much harder ownership obligations. || Authoritative document version / Indexing transformation / Searchable postings and fields / Rebuild from authoritative source

An embedding converts content into a numerical vector. A vector index finds nearby vectors, often approximately, to retrieve candidate documents with similar representations. Its architecture must balance recall, memory, updates, distance calculations, and filtering. ¶ Preserve the source document and version that produced each embedding. The index is derived and needs a rebuild path. Approximate search can miss relevant items, and a similarity score establishes neither factual truth nor access permission. Apply the required tenant and resource checks to candidates. Choose this structure for a demonstrated similarity-search need, rather than as a replacement for authoritative records or exact constraints. || Source and embedding version / Candidate vector search / Metadata and permission filter / Fetch authoritative source evidence

Object storage offers named blobs and metadata. Block storage offers addressable blocks. File storage offers a filesystem namespace and operations on files. These interfaces suit different callers. ¶ A PDF upload often fits object storage with separate application metadata. A database engine may require a filesystem backed by suitable block storage. Check the actual protocol and implementation rather than assuming category-wide guarantees. An uploaded object and a database reference are also separate changes: either can exist without the other after a failure. Track incomplete uploads, orphaned objects, and deletion so the application knows when a file is actually usable. || Client uploads object bytes / Object identity and checksum stored / Metadata commits reference / Cleanup handles abandoned objects

Checkout performs short transactions over current orders. A revenue report may scan years of payments. OLTP describes the first kind of transactional workload; OLAP describes the second kind of analytical work. ¶ A large report on the checkout database can compete for memory and I/O. Copying or transforming data into an analytical store can reduce that interference, but adds lag, lineage, and reconciliation requirements. One engine may serve both uses adequately at a smaller scale. Measure their interaction before introducing another system, and decide how current analytical results need to be. The labels explain workload differences, not mandatory product assignments. || Operational transaction commits / Data pipeline exports changes / Analytical layout serves aggregates / Freshness is explicitly bounded or observed

To display one customer's full record, it is convenient to read fields stored together in a row. To sum revenue over millions of records, reading only an amount column can avoid unrelated fields and support compression. ¶ Row-oriented storage groups fields by record; column-oriented storage groups values by field. Each changes locality and the cost of reads and updates. Modern engines can combine techniques, so inspect the actual access path. Storage layout does not replace a sound logical model or useful indexes. Choose it according to which bytes the important queries need to read and how frequently those values change. || Row: fields for record A / Column: amounts across records / Point lookup versus aggregate scan / Choose by measured access pattern

A customer's current address belongs in their profile. A completed order may also keep the address used for that shipment. This repetition preserves a historical fact; changing the profile should not rewrite the old order. ¶ Normalisation reduces duplicated facts and update anomalies. Denormalisation deliberately repeats or derives data for a purpose, often a faster read. State which meaning a copy has. A cached author name might be a historical snapshot or a value that should follow future edits. Without that distinction, developers cannot know whether a difference is correct history or stale data. Every maintained copy needs an update and recovery rule. || One authoritative customer profile / Order retains historical address fact / Read model may duplicate display name / Each copy has an update policy

An order has several lines. Each line belongs to one order, refers to a product identity, and retains the purchase-time values needed for its history. An entity-relationship model makes those identities and cardinalities explicit. ¶ Show keys, optional and required links, and the constraints that protect them. Also consider lifecycle: deleting a product must not remove the financial meaning of an old order. A diagram is more useful when it states these rules rather than merely joining table names. Translate the relevant constraints into the database or application so they are actually enforced. || Customer owns many orders / Order owns many lines / Line references product identity / Historical values survive catalog changes

An order line should not point to an order that does not exist. A suitable foreign key can enforce that relationship in a relational database. This is referential integrity. ¶ Decide deletion behaviour deliberately: automatically deleting dependent records may be wrong for historical payments. An application check before insertion can also race with deletion unless an atomic mechanism protects the relationship. Across separate services, one local foreign key cannot enforce a remote record's lifetime. Use stable identifiers, explicit ownership contracts, and reconciliation appropriate to the business rule. The integrity requirement stays important even when its enforcement crosses different storage boundaries. || Insert child reference / Database checks parent constraint / Atomic commit preserves local relation / Remote relation needs a different protocol

Payments records whether a charge was accepted. Analytics may keep a copy for reports, but should not rewrite that financial outcome. Data ownership identifies who may define and change each fact. ¶ If several services directly edit one table, they share transaction and release assumptions even if their source repositories are separate. Provide controlled read and change contracts, with only the permissions each consumer needs. Ownership also includes schema changes, incident response, retention, and recovery. Naming a table owner is a start; the design must give that owner a way to preserve its rules while others use the data. || Payment service owns outcome / Consumers use documented contract / Derived stores preserve source version / Owner manages correction and deletion

List the operations before assigning an engine. Profiles need particular lookups and edits. Financial entries need transaction and audit rules. Chat history needs bounded conversation/time reads. PDFs need large-byte storage and lifecycle management. ¶ Several of these needs may be met by one system. A relational or document model could both serve a profile, depending on its actual constraints. Add another engine when its capability solves a demonstrated problem. Each additional store introduces backup, permissions, migration, monitoring, and expertise costs. Prefer a storage choice justified by the operations it supports over one chosen merely to include every database category in the diagram. || Define query and write shapes / State required invariants / Estimate working set and growth / Select the smallest sufficient storage mix

A document's lifecycle includes its original bytes, extracted fields, indexes, archives, and recoverable copies. Deleting only the live row may leave other copies usable. ¶ Define purpose, retention, expiration, archival, and deletion behaviour from the start. Dropping time partitions can efficiently expire old data; deleting one user's records needs identity-aware propagation. A minimal deletion marker may be needed to prevent old events from recreating removed data, but that marker can itself retain personal information. Keep only what its purpose needs. Test restoration from an old backup so required deletion decisions are reapplied instead of silently reversed. || Record purpose and expiry / Delete or archive authoritative data / Propagate to derived stores / Reapply deletion after recovery

A shop adds a search engine because its current queries cannot meet a demonstrated relevance need. It now uses different stores for different workloads: polyglot persistence. ¶ The extra capability comes with indexing lag, rebuilds, permissions, schema changes, and another recovery procedure. A change across two stores is not automatically one transaction. Identify which record is authoritative and which representation can be rebuilt, then monitor progress between them. Compare the complete operating cost with the benefit of the new query path. Keeping fewer engines can be an advantage when one already meets the requirements. || Add specialized read engine / Define source of truth / Maintain and reconcile derived state / Pay operating and recovery cost''')

put(10, '''A lookup by task identifier looks simple to its caller. Inside the database, it may traverse an index, fetch pages into memory, check which row version is visible, and return a result. Each step consumes a different resource. ¶ Following this path helps explain why one query waits for disk while another uses CPU or waits for a lock. The operating system and storage device also affect the result. Inspect the query plan and resource behaviour before replacing the database or adding servers. An improvement is easier to justify when it addresses the expensive step rather than an assumed weakness of the database category. || Query reaches engine / Planner selects access path / Pages and versions are read / Visible records become results

Many database engines organise storage into pages containing records or index entries. A buffer pool keeps useful pages in memory. Finding a page there avoids some device I/O, although execution and synchronisation still take work. ¶ Suppose a query needs a million scattered pages. Extra web servers may simply send more random reads to the same storage. The actively used pages and their locality matter more than row count alone. Inspect what the query touches and how much fits in memory. Page size, layout, and replacement policy differ by engine, so use its actual implementation when explaining detailed behaviour. || Record stored on page / Index locates candidate page / Buffer pool hit or disk read / Visibility check selects record version

An ordered index helps find keys 20 through 35 without checking every record. B-tree-family structures keep ordered separators along a bounded-height path. B+tree designs commonly keep records or references at leaves and support continuing a range through neighbouring leaves. ¶ A hash index instead targets equality lookup under its implementation; it does not supply ordered range traversal. Choose by predicates, ordering, engine support, and update cost. Even a short tree path can require page reads, and returning many matching rows still takes time. Algorithmic complexity is useful, but query cost also depends on storage locality and the amount of output. || Root chooses key range / Internal page narrows interval / Leaf locates key 20 / Neighboring leaf supplies range results

An LSM-based engine collects writes in memory, flushes sorted runs, and later merges them through compaction. This can turn many small changes into more sequential storage work. ¶ Reads may consult several structures, and compaction writes some data again. Write amplification compares physical bytes written with the logical change size. Its value depends on the layout and workload. Compaction also competes for bandwidth and can cause stalls when it falls behind. Observe sustained ingestion and tail latency, rather than only a short burst that fits in memory. Write-oriented organisation changes costs; it does not remove limits or make reads free. || Append durable intent / Update in-memory sorted state / Flush immutable sorted run / Compaction rewrites and merges runs

A SQL query describes the desired result. The planner chooses how to obtain it, using statistics and estimated costs. EXPLAIN shows the selected plan; an execution-analysis form can show actual work, but may execute the statement and its side effects. ¶ Suppose the planner expects ten rows and finds a million. A join strategy that looked cheap can become expensive. Compare estimates with actual row counts, filters, sorts, scans, and buffer activity. Statistics or a query rewrite may be the right first correction. Check the selected engine's command behaviour before analysing a statement that changes data. || SQL expresses result / Statistics estimate cardinality / Planner chooses joins and scans / Compare actual rows with estimates

A tenant-scoped list query filters by tenant and sorts by time and identifier. An index on (tenant_id,created_at,id) may support that access path, depending on the engine and query. ¶ An index also consumes space and must be updated on writes. Redundant or unused indexes impose that cost without helping the important queries. A larger covering index can avoid some record fetches but reduce memory locality. Test representative values and distributions: a small uniform dataset may conceal a tenant with most of the rows. Select indexes to serve demonstrated operations, then inspect the resulting plan and write cost. || Query filters tenant / Index orders time and ID / Read bounded matching range / Writes maintain index entries too

A transaction transfers an amount between two accounts. Atomicity means its related database changes are accepted together or abandoned together. Isolation controls what concurrent transactions may observe. Durability concerns keeping committed data under the stated configuration and failure assumptions. ¶ Consistency, the C in ACID, concerns preserving the required invariants when application logic and constraints correctly express them. The engine cannot infer every business rule. Atomically transferring the wrong amount still produces the wrong business outcome. ACID separates useful properties so we can ask the right question of each. A transaction's existence alone does not specify its isolation level or validate its calculation. || Begin transaction / Validate declared invariant / Apply related changes / Commit under isolation and durability contract

The application changes rows and asks the database to commit. Only after a successful commit should it report success if that report promises a saved result. A rollback abandons uncommitted transaction work. ¶ Now lose the connection while commit is being decided. The caller may be unsure whether the database accepted the transaction. Recovery needs an operation identity or another way to find the outcome. Also separate local rollback from external actions: rolling back SQL does not reverse a provider charge already made over HTTP. Keep such uncertainty explicit rather than blindly replaying an entire operation containing unprotected remote effects. || Update local transaction / Request commit / Connection may fail around result / Reconcile uncertain outcome by identity

A database may commit while some modified data pages are still awaiting their final storage writes. With write-ahead logging, it first persists the recovery information needed to reconstruct the accepted work under its protocol. ¶ On recovery, the engine uses the log to recover the appropriate state. Checkpoints reduce recovery work by advancing what has been written; they are not backups. Acknowledgment durability depends on sync and replication settings. Losing the only device that holds both log and data can still lose everything. Logging protects particular recovery steps, while independent copies and correct application changes address different failure classes. || Change produces log record / Required log persistence precedes data / Commit point follows configured policy / Recovery replays appropriate logged work

A reader starts while another transaction updates a row. With multiversion concurrency control, or MVCC, the engine can keep multiple versions and choose the one visible under the reader's snapshot rules. ¶ This reduces some direct reader/writer interference, but the snapshot contract still matters. A long reader can retain old versions and delay cleanup. Two transactions can read the same multi-row rule, change different rows, and together violate it. That is possible write skew under suitable snapshot semantics. Learn the selected isolation and conflict rules; the presence of versions alone does not establish serializable execution. || Reader snapshot established / Writer creates new row version / Reader sees its allowed version / Cleanup waits for relevant readers

Two buyers see seat version seven. Each tries UPDATE ... WHERE version=7, changing the owner and advancing the version. After one succeeds, the other's condition no longer matches, so it changes zero rows. ¶ This is optimistic concurrency control: validate the state assumption when applying the change. Zero rows means conflict, requiring a fresh read and business decision. Pessimistic locking coordinates before conflicting work instead. Locks can wait or deadlock; optimistic retries can waste effort under heavy contention. Choose according to conflict frequency and operation cost, and protect the whole invariant rather than treating every affected-row result as success. || Read seat version 7 / Compute proposed reservation / Conditional update expects version 7 / One winner advances version to 8

A dirty read observes another transaction's uncommitted change. A nonrepeatable read sees a row change between two reads. A phantom changes the set of rows matching a repeated query—for example, a fourth reservation appears after a query first found three. ¶ These names describe histories worth distinguishing, but whether they occur depends on the chosen engine and isolation level. Write the problematic sequence in order and check the documented behaviour. Do not assume equal isolation names mean identical implementations. A test of one row read also cannot establish every predicate or write-conflict guarantee needed by the application. || Read matching reservation set / Concurrent transaction changes data / Repeat row or predicate read / Allowed result depends on isolation

Two doctors are on call. Each transaction checks that the other doctor remains on call, then marks its own doctor off. If both see the original snapshot, they update different rows but leave nobody on call. ¶ This is write skew: individually plausible updates violate a shared rule. A lost update instead overwrites another writer's result. More generally, a serialization anomaly has no equivalent valid serial ordering. Protect the actual invariant with suitable serialization, coordinated locking, or a shared conflict point. Row-level protection alone does not necessarily cover a condition spanning several rows. || A sees B on call / B sees A on call / A turns itself off / B turns itself off: invariant fails

The selected engine's isolation implementation is part of the application contract. In PostgreSQL 18, ordinary Read Committed queries use statement snapshots. Repeatable Read gives a transaction snapshot but can still allow serialization anomalies. Serializable adds conflict handling that can reject a transaction. ¶ These rules are version- and statement-sensitive; other engines can differ. When a transaction must retry, restart the whole affected transaction using fresh reads, with bounded attempts. Repeating only its last statement can retain assumptions from a snapshot that is no longer valid. Keep externally visible effects safe under replay as well. An isolation name is a starting point for reading the actual guarantees. || Select documented engine and level / Construct anomaly history / Observe commit or conflict / Retry entire transaction when required

Serializable transactions produce a committed result equivalent to some serial transaction order. That order need not be the same as wall-clock order between separate callers. ¶ Linearizability concerns operations respecting real-time precedence, as if each took effect at one point between its invocation and reply. Strict serializability combines real-time constraints with transaction serialization. Keep these properties separate when stating a requirement. A serializable transaction may abort and need retry, so protect external effects in any replayed code. Stronger concurrency guarantees help preserve a correctly expressed rule; they cannot correct a wrong fee calculation or supply every desired availability property. || Transactions execute concurrently / Engine rejects incompatible history / Committed effect has serial equivalent / Real-time order is a separate condition

Assume ten database connections, each held for an average of 100 ms. The simple fully busy service bound is 10/0.1 = 100 operations per second before other constraints and variability. ¶ A pool limits connections but also creates acquisition waits. Increasing its size can add contention instead of throughput. Measure hold time, lock waits, idle transactions, and queue age. If callers retain a connection while waiting for an unrelated HTTP service, shorten that ownership interval where the transaction allows it. Include acquisition in the request deadline. Pool capacity is useful only when the database can perform the work it admits. || Borrow bounded connection / Execute short transaction / Release promptly / Observe acquisition and lock waits

Reserving a seat and inserting the corresponding local reservation must agree. If one commits without the other, the records no longer describe one valid outcome. Put both statements inside a deliberate transaction boundary. ¶ Check what the libraries actually do. An ORM save call does not establish that every related action shares the same transaction. Across stores or remote services, use an explicit coordination protocol and reconciliation where needed. Keep a local transaction short while covering the required invariant. Do not let a returned lazy result depend on resources already closed when the operation's scope ended. || Decrement available inventory / Insert reservation record / Commit both local changes / Failure rolls back the local unit

A backup job reports success. We still need to know whether its data can be decrypted, restored, and used by the application. Test restoration with the required schema, credentials, and business checks, and measure how long it takes. ¶ Replication maintains live copies and may repeat an accidental deletion. Backups preserve recoverable history under their retention policy. Point-in-time recovery also needs the appropriate log history. State which acknowledged writes can be recovered after each failure. Owning a backup file is useful evidence of a file, but the recovery promise requires a tested procedure and suitable independent storage. || Capture consistent backup / Retain required recovery logs / Restore into clean environment / Verify data and recovery objectives''')

put(11, '''A product description is read thousands of times between edits. Reusing a stored response can avoid repeating the database lookup or calculation. This reuse is why caching can help. ¶ Assume a cache hit takes 1 ms, a miss takes 50 ms, and 90% of requests hit. The simple average service time is 0.9×1 + 0.1×50 = 5.9 ms, excluding queueing and other work. Compare this saving with memory, lookup, and maintenance costs. A result used only once may be a poor cache candidate. Hit ratio also does not establish tail latency: slow misses and queues can still dominate some users' waits. || Repeated product lookup / Hit returns cached representation / Miss reaches authoritative store / Benefit depends on reuse and miss cost

A user repeatedly opens the same product: that is temporal locality. Reading neighbouring records together illustrates spatial locality. A working set is the data actively needed during a relevant interval. ¶ A cache much smaller than that set may continually evict entries just before they are reused. A large scan can also displace useful entries under some policies. Measure bytes and object sizes as well as key counts, and distinguish frequently used data from the full historical collection. Preloading every object can fill memory with cold content. Admission rules decide what deserves a place before eviction decides what must leave. || Hot working set / Bounded cache memory / Admission and eviction decisions / Observe churn and useful hit rate

Two application instances each keep a price in memory. One has price 10 and another price 12. Their in-process caches provide fast access but create separate copies whose freshness needs coordination. ¶ A distributed cache provides a shared network-accessible layer, adding encoding, connection, and failure costs. Choose by reuse patterns, duplicated memory, and required behaviour when a copy is stale or unavailable. Ordinary cached stock counts should not become the sole authority for exclusive allocation. Placing the cache elsewhere changes its access and failure boundaries; it does not by itself resolve the application's consistency rule. || App A caches price 10 / Database commits price 12 / App B sees refreshed value / App A may remain stale

A page can be answered by a browser cache, a CDN, an application cache, or a database buffer pool. Each stores a different kind of copy. Database pages and a rendered account response also have different keys and update rules. ¶ Draw the layers actually used and specify freshness at each. Invalidating an application object does not clear a browser's response. A public edge cache with a poorly scoped key can expose personal data. When diagnosing a stale result, identify which layer answered and which version it held. “The cache was cleared” is incomplete unless it names the particular copy involved. || Browser representation / Shared edge representation / Application object copy / Database page and committed version

With cache-aside, application code loads from the source after a miss and populates the cache. Read-through places that loading behind the cache interface. Write-through includes backing-store work in the write path; write-behind delays it. Exact ordering varies by implementation. ¶ Trace failures explicitly. A volatile write-behind cache can lose acknowledged changes before backing persistence. Cache-aside also has a race: a reader fetches version one, a writer commits version two and invalidates, then the delayed reader inserts version one. Where freshness requires stronger behaviour, use an appropriate version or coordination protocol. A pattern name alone does not resolve these event sequences. || Cache miss reads old value / Writer commits new value / Writer invalidates cache / Delayed reader can repopulate old value

A cache receives a value that was already five minutes old and gives it a one-minute TTL. The copy may then remain six minutes behind its source. TTL describes residence after insertion, rather than necessarily the age of the fact. ¶ Expiration also differs from eviction due to memory pressure. Choose lifetimes using tolerated staleness and refresh behaviour, and consider client-held copies. Adding jitter can spread expirations that would otherwise happen together. For a correctness-critical action, revalidate against the authority when required. A short display-cache TTL is useful only if it matches the promise made about that display. || Value observed at source time T / Cached later at T plus delay / TTL begins at insertion / Source age includes both delay and TTL

A writer updates the source and deletes a cache entry. A delayed reader can still repopulate it from an earlier read, leaving an old value after the invalidation. Simply deleting after writing does not prevent every race. ¶ Versioned entries, conditional population, or source-version checks can support a stronger protocol. Observe delayed or repeated invalidation events and their progress. Choose freshness per operation. Showing an older price may be acceptable if checkout verifies the actual price; allocating the last item from an old count can oversell it. Invalidation must serve the business rule, rather than merely keep copies approximately similar. || Authoritative version increases / Invalidation or update is emitted / Cached copy compares version / Critical operation validates authoritative state

At capacity three, access A,B,C,A,D. Ideal LRU treats the second access to A as recent and evicts B. FIFO preserves insertion order and evicts A. LFU favours frequently used entries but also needs a tie-breaking rule. ¶ Real cache policies may approximate these ideals. Test the actual policy with representative object sizes and request patterns. Eviction decides what leaves; admission decides what enters. Neither can make an arbitrarily large working set fit into memory. A policy that performs well on one tiny fixture may behave poorly during scans or changing popularity, so measure the workload it is meant to serve. || Capacity: 3 entries / Access A, B, C, A / Insert D / LRU removes B; FIFO removes A

A single product becomes extremely popular. Even a large cache cluster can overload the one owner serving that key. Adding nodes mainly gives other keys more capacity unless the design also distributes this key's traffic. ¶ For reads, local copies, replicas, or coalescing repeated work may help, with an explicit freshness policy. For writes, determine whether the value can be split or combined safely. An aggregate counter may allow distributed increments; exclusive stock allocation needs a stronger rule. Identify whether the pressure is transfer volume, recomputation, object size, or serialised updates before selecting a remedy. || One product becomes viral / One cache owner receives most reads / Add suitable read copies or coalescing / Preserve authoritative allocation semantics

Many requests miss the same key and all recompute it: a cache stampede. Repeated requests for nonexistent keys are often called penetration. Broad expiry or cache loss can cause an avalanche of origin traffic. ¶ Use controls suited to the cause. Single-flight loading combines concurrent work for one key. Negative caching can remember a genuine absence. TTL jitter spreads expiry, while bounded refresh and origin admission protect the source. A refresh lock needs its own failure and expiry behaviour. Test cache loss: allowing every application instance to rebuild everything at once can overload the database that remains responsible for the real data. || Many readers miss at once / Duplicate origin queries accumulate / Coalesce refresh for one key / Bound total origin work during recovery

A client repeatedly asks for a product that does not exist. A short-lived “not found” entry can prevent repeated source lookups. This is negative caching. ¶ Distinguish absence from failure. A temporarily unreachable database has not established that the product is missing. Likewise, one user's denied access must not poison another user's cache entry. Scope keys correctly and use deliberate lifetimes. If the product is created just after its absence is cached, it may stay invisible until expiry or invalidation. The policy should state whether that delay is acceptable and how newly created records become visible. || Product P absent / Cache absence for bounded interval / Product P is created / Invalidate absence or wait defined TTL

A cached product description expires while its source is slow. Stale-while-revalidate can return an allowed older copy and refresh it in the background. The user avoids the immediate wait, and combined refresh work can protect the origin. ¶ Define separate ages for fresh data, permitted stale data, and unusable data. Repeated refresh failures should not silently extend stale serving forever. A description may tolerate this arrangement; an old payment reply cannot authorise a new charge. Make degradation visible where appropriate and preserve authoritative checks for operations whose correctness depends on current state. Faster responses have value only within the agreed freshness rule. || Value becomes stale / Reader receives permitted old copy / One background refresh runs / Hard age limit eventually forbids serving

New application instances start with empty caches. Warming loads likely useful entries before normal demand; prefetching predicts what will be needed soon. Both spend bandwidth and memory before a user actually asks. ¶ Load a measured working set gradually rather than scanning the entire database from every new instance. Check whether prefetched values are used and whether they evict more valuable entries. Staggering a rollout, sharing a cache, or limiting admission may solve a cold-start problem more simply. Stop obsolete speculative work. The useful question is how much future work was avoided, rather than how many entries were loaded. || New instance starts / Warm bounded hot set / Observe useful prefetch ratio / Avoid coordinated origin overload

A set can track identifiers, a sorted set can organise scores or timestamps, and a hash can group fields. Redis-like systems expose such structures with operations suited to particular tasks. ¶ Choose by the operations needed, then examine their atomic scope. One atomic command does not make an arbitrary sequence of commands atomic. A script or transaction may protect the local sequence, subject to the selected system's rules and key placement. Expiry, persistence, and replica behaviour remain separate decisions. A convenient list or stream is not automatically a durable queue with the required acknowledgment and recovery semantics. || Select structure for operation / Keep compound invariant atomic / Bound memory and expiry / Check persistence and cluster semantics

An origin shield collects edge misses before they reach the source, reducing duplicate origin work. A CDN places permitted representations near users. Both depend on correct keys and sharing rules. ¶ Decide how query parameters, headers, identities, and content versions affect the representation. A signed URL is a capability with a scope and lifetime, not permission to ignore private-data policy. Purge propagation follows the provider contract and may not remove copies already kept by clients. Immutable versioned URLs can simplify public asset updates. Trace every remaining copy when immediate revocation matters; proximity and bandwidth savings do not establish access correctness. || Client to nearby edge / Edge miss reaches shield / Shield miss reaches origin / Versioned content separates representations

Partitioning assigns different keys to different owners. Replication makes additional copies under a protocol. A distributed cache may use both, but failover can still lose, evict, or expose delayed entries. ¶ Specify whether the application falls back, serves permitted stale data, or rejects when the cache fails. Stable hashing can reduce key remapping during membership changes, while leaving hot-key traffic uneven. Keep source and cache recovery capacity explicit. The real peak may arrive when a cache cluster restarts and every missing value needs reconstruction. Test that scenario rather than sizing the origin only for a warm-cache hit ratio. || Key maps to partition / Partition may have replicas / Failover can expose misses or lag / Origin recovery budget remains bounded

A cache adds a lookup, encoding, memory use, and copy-maintenance work. If results are rarely reused, those costs can exceed the work saved. If its key omits authorisation scope, the cache can also serve the wrong user's result. ¶ Test with the cache empty and unavailable. A warm cache can hide insufficient source capacity until the day it disappears. Sometimes a better query or index removes the bottleneck with fewer freshness and recovery obligations. Keep caching where measured reuse and the agreed correctness policy justify it. Removing an unhelpful cache can simplify both performance and reasoning. || Measure saved origin work / Count invalidation and operating cost / Test cold and failed cache / Remove cache when it worsens the contract''')

put(12, '''A busy application can run on a larger machine or on more machines. Vertical scaling increases one node's resources. Horizontal scaling adds nodes and distributes suitable work. ¶ Neither guarantees proportional throughput. A larger database machine can postpone distribution work, while extra application instances can share independent computation. A common lock or downstream database may remain the limit. Compare startup delay, failure impact, cost, and the work needed to operate the result. Profile the bottleneck first, make the smallest justified change, then measure again. The limiting resource can move after a successful improvement. || Identify saturated resource / Increase node capacity or add owners / Shared bottleneck may remain / Measure completed useful work again

A request reaches a replacement web instance. Can that instance handle it using the same authenticated identity and stored state? A stateless application avoids keeping required per-client conversation state only in a particular instance between requests. ¶ It still uses state elsewhere. Local sessions or accepted memory-only jobs can prevent transparent replacement. Move required state to a suitable owner or specify affinity and recovery. Check compatible configuration, state access, and duplicate effects across instances. Distributing requests freely works only when these conditions hold; labelling a service “stateless” does not establish them by itself. || Request reaches instance A / Required state has external owner / Next request reaches instance B / B reconstructs context under contract

Round-robin distributes backend selections in turn. Least-connections uses active connections as a load clue. Weights can represent different capacities, and hashing can keep related traffic together. ¶ The best clue depends on the workload. One expensive request can occupy an instance longer than many cheap requests, while a long-lived connection can carry changing traffic. Inspect per-instance saturation and waiting instead of only counting requests. Client-side and server-side balancing also place discovery and retry decisions in different components. Choose a policy that reflects useful load, and remember that routing cannot add capacity to a shared downstream bottleneck. || Choose backend by policy / Observe actual work and queue / Avoid unhealthy or overloaded target / Rebalance without multiplying retries

A process responds to a simple ping, but cannot serve real traffic. Another process can serve a reduced response even though a shared optional dependency is down. Health checks need to reflect the routing decision they control. ¶ Liveness concerns whether a process should be restarted. Readiness concerns whether it should receive traffic under the chosen policy. Requiring every dependency in every readiness check can remove all instances during one shared outage. Use bounded, meaningful checks and deliberate degradation. A traffic switch also needs valid state and connection behaviour; routing to a new instance does not recover work that existed only in the old one's memory. || Probe readiness / Remove target when policy says unsafe / Route new traffic elsewhere / State recovery remains a separate obligation

Sticky sessions send a client's requests to the same instance. This can support local session state or improve cache reuse, but also creates an affinity that affects balancing and recovery. ¶ If that instance fails, decide how the client reconnects and how required state is recovered. Heavy users may create uneven load. For replaceable web instances, externally owned sessions can remove some affinity needs. Where sticking is deliberate, document the consequences rather than relying on it invisibly. Affinity can be an optimisation or a constraint; the authoritative location and lifetime of session state still need a clear design. || Client assigned instance A / Later requests retain affinity / A fails or drains / Recover session or reconnect explicitly

A worker queue's oldest job is getting older even while CPU looks moderate. Scaling solely on CPU might miss the delay users care about. The signal should reflect the constrained work: CPU for computation, queue age for job delay, or connections for some waiting workloads. ¶ Autoscaling also has startup time, minimums, and maximums. Use stabilisation to avoid repeatedly adding and removing instances, and cap spending and downstream pressure. Request-count scaling assumes comparable request costs. Keep existing headroom and admission controls for bursts that arrive before new capacity is ready. Automation reacts to a limit; it does not remove the reaction interval. || Metric crosses threshold / Controller requests capacity / Instances start and warm / Admission limits protect the delay

A primary can copy writes to a replica before or after replying. In asynchronous replication, a reply may precede receipt on the replica. If that replica is promoted after losing the primary, recent acknowledged writes may be absent. ¶ Synchronous arrangements wait for a defined replica milestone and can increase delay or block during communication failure. State exactly which copies and milestones acknowledgment requires. Replication can improve reading or continuity while adding lag and recovery rules. Independent failure placement also matters: two copies on one lost disk provide little protection. A copy count is useful only alongside its protocol and failure assumptions. || Leader accepts write / Protocol determines required acknowledgments / Replica may lag / Promotion must honor the durability contract

A dataset can be divided into partitions. When separate storage owners hold those portions, this is commonly called sharding. The key determines where data lives and which queries need several owners. ¶ Tenant-based placement may keep each tenant's data together while leaving one large tenant hot. Cross-shard uniqueness, joins, transactions, and movement need additional design. Keep a routing and relocation procedure, and name where invariants are enforced. Add this complexity when a measured storage or write problem justifies it. A large user count alone does not identify a partition key or establish that one database has reached its limit. || Choose ownership key / Route records to partition / Query may cross owners / Rebalance with explicit movement protocol

Range partitioning keeps ordered keys together, which can help scans and expiration by time. But if every new event goes into today's range, that owner receives most writes. Hash partitioning spreads keys while making ordered range reads less local. ¶ A time bucket plus a spread dimension can distribute ingestion, at the cost of reading several partitions for one time range. State that fan-out explicitly. Neither technique automatically splits one extremely popular key. Choose by the writes, reads, retention, and skew the application actually has, rather than using “balanced storage” as a substitute for measuring traffic. || Range: related keys together / Hash: keys spread by function / Hot key remains hot / Query locality and write balance trade off

Modulo routing can move many keys when the owner count changes. Consistent hashing uses a stable placement, often a ring with virtual positions, to limit reassignment. Rendezvous hashing instead scores each key-owner pair and selects the highest eligible score. ¶ Both aim to keep much of the old assignment while membership changes. Neither guarantees equal request traffic: one key can be far more popular than others. Clients must agree about membership and hash behaviour, and data still needs safe movement and replication. Changing the hash function also changes placement, so treat it as a migration rather than a harmless implementation detail. || Stable owner set / Map key using ring or scores / Add owner and remap subset / Transfer data and preserve request routing

Moving a partition while requests continue is a coordination operation. Copy its data from a defined starting point, capture changes made during the copy, validate the destination, and establish when it becomes the owner. ¶ Readers and writers need a consistent way to discover that transition, such as a routing epoch. Throttle copying so it leaves capacity for users. Keep the old copy until the new ownership and recovery conditions permit removal. Plan rollback and reconciliation too. A move that deletes the source before proving the destination current can turn a capacity improvement into data loss. || Copy partition snapshot / Apply concurrent changes / Switch ownership epoch / Retire old copy after validation

Fleet averages look healthy, but one partition handles 70% of writes. More cold partitions do little for that owner. Inspect requests, bytes, and delays by partition to find the actual skew. ¶ A range split helps only if the traffic can divide. One hot entity may require read copies, batching, or a redesigned ownership rule. Randomising writes can spread append pressure but force reads to combine more partitions. Determine whether the issue is volume, size, or a serialised invariant. Choose a remedy that changes that pressure, rather than repeatedly buying capacity elsewhere in the cluster. || Fleet average appears healthy / One partition receives 70% of writes / Its queue determines tail latency / Split or redesign the specific hot path

A user updates a profile on the primary, then reads from a replica that has not applied the change. The next response shows the old value. Read/write splitting needs a freshness policy. ¶ Route critical reads to a sufficiently current owner, track session progress where supported, or explicitly allow delayed results. Classifying by SQL verb alone is unsafe: a SELECT may acquire a lock or participate in a transaction requiring current state. Read replicas can distribute appropriate reads while writes remain elsewhere. Make the operation's required observations part of the routing decision, rather than assuming every read can go to any replica. || Write commits at primary / Replica has older log position / Replica read can be stale / Critical read uses sufficient freshness

A source has accepted changes through position 100, but a replica has applied only through 90. This position difference describes part of its replication lag. The transport may have received newer records that still wait to be applied. ¶ Measure source, receipt, and apply progress where the system supports them. A small average can hide one stalled replica. Time-based lag estimates also need interpretation when traffic varies. Promotion must respect the acknowledgment and possible-loss contract, not just happen quickly. For a user needing read-your-writes, route according to the required version or another supported mechanism rather than relying on the fleet's average freshness. || Source commits position 100 / Replica applied position 95 / Read may miss positions 96–100 / Promotion and routing inspect progress

A database proxy can route clients or reuse backend connections. Some implementations multiplex several client sessions onto fewer database connections. This can change assumptions about session variables, temporary tables, or long transactions. ¶ Check which state must stay attached to a particular backend. Document where identity, failover, and routing decisions occur, and size the proxy itself. It can become another constrained or failed component. Pooling does not remove transaction limits, and a failed call cannot always be transparently repeated after a connection switch. Choose proxy features against the application's actual session and operation contract. || Client borrows proxy connection / Proxy selects backend session / Transaction pins required state / Release only when session contract allows

Ten application instances each open a pool of twenty database connections. Adding ten more instances can double connection demand even if the database has no more capacity. Waiting callers may then accumulate both in application pools and inside the database. ¶ Bound the aggregate, reserve recovery capacity, and include acquisition waits in deadlines. A request waiting on a database lock may hold a connection while using little CPU. Measure hold time and contention before increasing pool sizes. Scaling the admission points can worsen an outage when it increases pressure on the shared resource already causing the wait. || App instances multiply pools / Database connection budget is fixed / Borrowers wait and time out / Bound aggregate pressure and shorten ownership

A request spends most of its time waiting for one database query. Adding web instances is unlikely to fix that path; a query or index change may help more. Another service may instead be limited by independent CPU work and benefit from workers. ¶ Trace useful completions and waiting through the system. Compare CPU, memory, disk, network, connections, and lock contention with offered load and queues. High utilisation is a clue, not proof of the business bottleneck. Change one material factor and measure again. This links an intervention to evidence and shows whether the limit moved elsewhere. || Trace request critical path / Inspect resource and queue evidence / Change demonstrated limiting stage / Re-measure useful throughput and tails

A queue can absorb a brief burst when workers later catch up. If arrival stays above completion, the backlog keeps growing. Longer waiting can make a request useless even before memory runs out. ¶ Bound both queue length and age, prioritise required work, and reject or shed work that cannot meet its deadline. A request with a two-second deadline should not be accepted silently behind ten seconds of work. Explain the overload result so callers do not immediately create a retry storm. A queue is a place for controlled waiting, not a way to turn insufficient sustained capacity into sufficient capacity. || Arrival exceeds service rate / Waiting time grows / Deadline usefulness expires / Bound admission before resources exhaust

Two application containers run on one host. They survive a single process failure but not the host's failure. A single point of failure is a dependency whose loss defeats the required service, which may be a machine, router, credential source, or operator-controlled procedure. ¶ Inspect independent failure domains rather than counting process boxes. Redundancy also needs detection, valid state, surviving capacity, and failover. Check recovery dependencies: a data path may be replicated while every replacement instance needs one unavailable configuration service to start. Trace what must work during a failure, not only what runs during normal operation. || Two replicas share one host / Host fails / Both replicas disappear / Separate correlated failure domains deliberately

A second region can help locality or continuity, but it changes where writes are accepted and how copies agree. Active-passive keeps one current authority and needs promotion and recovery rules. Active-active needs a precise coordination or conflict policy for concurrent activity. ¶ Waiting across regions adds propagation time. Copying asynchronously reduces that wait but may lose recent acknowledged changes during failover. Begin with tolerated interruption, data loss, and locality requirements. Choose write authority and read freshness per operation. A single “multi-region” label cannot establish which work continues during a partition or which state survives it. || Select regional write ownership / Define acknowledgment across regions / Partition changes allowed behavior / Failover preserves stated recovery contract

A product description can be copied to many readers with an allowed freshness delay. Allocating the last seat needs exclusive ownership of that seat. These operations have different scaling options. ¶ Read scaling often uses suitable replicas or caches. Write scaling must divide, reduce, or batch ownership work while preserving its invariants. One hot account or global sequential allocator can remain serialised despite many shards. First remove unnecessary coordination or indexes where justified. Commutative counters may support independent updates; exclusive reservations need a protocol that prevents incompatible winners. Scale the actual rule, rather than assuming reads and writes benefit from the same arrangement. || Reads reuse representations / Writes alter authoritative state / Partition or batch suitable writes / Preserve the actual invariant boundary

Ten small nodes introduce repeated baseline memory, extra connections, transfer, monitoring, and operating work. They may or may not cost less than one larger node for the required workload. ¶ Compare candidates with equal service targets and spare capacity for the same failures. Include startup, cache warming, migration, and recovery. Managed services can exchange provider charges for some staffing work, but the application still owns its correctness. Cost per successful business outcome is more useful than cost per attempted request when retries differ. Identify the bottleneck before purchasing capacity that cannot increase useful completions. || Same workload and objectives / Include failure and migration headroom / Count network and operating costs / Compare cost per successful outcome''')

put(13, '''A processing job needs both a PDF's bytes and information about its owner and progress. Store immutable binary content separately when its access and lifecycle differ from the mutable job record. ¶ The record should identify a particular object version, its size or checksum, and ownership. A row pointing to a missing or incomplete object is a broken workflow even if both storage services individually operated correctly. Track upload state and clean up abandoned bytes. Verify the chosen object store's guarantees; “object storage” names an interface category rather than a universal consistency or durability contract. || Upload object bytes / Verify object identity and size / Commit metadata reference / Process only verified complete input

Instead of sending a large upload through application workers, the service can issue a narrowly scoped authorisation for the client to stream directly to storage. The application still owns permission, quotas, metadata, and the decision that a file is ready. ¶ A client may abandon the upload or send different bytes than expected. Validate completion, size, and ownership before starting processing. Expire unused upload intents and reconcile unreferenced objects. A signed upload URL is a capability: its scope and lifetime determine what its holder may do. Grant only the intended object operation, rather than broad access to the storage collection. || Create upload intent / Client sends bytes to storage / Server validates completion / Durable job becomes ready

An interrupted large upload should not always restart from byte zero. Multipart upload divides it into named parts and has a separate completion step. Resumable upload remembers confirmed offsets or parts so the client can continue. ¶ Specify how retries identify a part and what happens if the client's source file changes. Acknowledging every part does not by itself establish that the assembled object is the right complete file. Verify the final content under the chosen protocol. Provider entity tags are not universally content hashes. Remove expired incomplete sessions, or abandoned parts can create storage growth that no visible completed file explains. || Upload intent and parts manifest / Confirm parts 1, 2 and 4 / Retry missing part 3 / Complete and verify assembled object

A downloaded file differs from the uploaded bytes. A checksum can help detect corruption according to its algorithm's properties. A suitable cryptographic digest can also identify content under stated collision assumptions. ¶ Content addressing derives an identity from that digest, supporting immutable references or deduplication. Verify the actual stored or received bytes rather than hashing only a client's claim. Matching content does not establish permission to access it or that the content is safe. Cross-tenant deduplication can reveal information through existence checks, so design its scope carefully. Integrity, identity, authorisation, and content safety are related but separate decisions. || Hash actual uploaded bytes / Compare expected digest / Store immutable content identity / Authorize every reference independently

A user replaces a document after an extraction completed. If the same input identifier silently refers to new bytes, we can no longer explain which file produced the earlier result. ¶ Give binary versions stable identities. Keep annotations, classifications, attempts, and review status in metadata that names those versions. When the input changes, create a new version and decide which derived results must be recomputed. Retain or remove historical artifacts according to policy. This separation lets the application explain an output without treating mutable processing state as part of the immutable original file. || Immutable file version F1 / Metadata points to F1 / Extraction output records F1 and model M / New bytes become F2

Consider D1 = “blue car” and D2 = “blue bike.” An inverted index records that blue occurs in D1 and D2, while car occurs only in D1. A query can use these postings to find candidates without scanning every document's text. ¶ Tokenisation and normalisation affect which terms are recorded. Updates also need document identity and version: an older extraction must not overwrite a newer representation. Keep recoverable sources and transformation information so the index can be rebuilt. Its retrieval structure has its own freshness and access obligations, even when the original document is stored correctly elsewhere. || D1: blue car / D2: blue bike / blue maps to D1 and D2 / car maps only to D1

A document can be uploaded successfully while indexing fails. Treat extraction, validation, enrichment, indexing, and publication as distinguishable stages so the application can report where progress stopped. ¶ Each retry should name its input version and intended output, rather than create another searchable document blindly. Give malformed or repeatedly failing files a bounded retry and review path. Publication must also respect current access policy; an old “public” flag copied into an artifact may no longer be valid. A useful completed state names the source and index version whose required work actually finished. || Source version ready / Extract and validate text / Index with stable document ID / Publish searchable state and version

A new analyser needs a fresh search index while users continue searching. Build a new generation from a defined snapshot or source position, then apply changes that occurred during the build. ¶ Include deletions as well as inserts and edits. Validate results before switching a serving alias or routing version. Keep the old usable generation until rollback and compatibility conditions permit removal. Equal document counts do not establish equal versions, permissions, or contents. The transition is safe only when readers know which representation is authoritative and new writes are not lost between the initial copy and the switch. || Build index generation B / Replay changes after snapshot / Validate versions and deletions / Switch serving pointer from A to B

A nightly batch can recompute yesterday's report from a bounded input. A streaming processor handles continuing input and records progress as it arrives. Choose by the required freshness and recomputation behaviour. ¶ Streaming can reduce delay, but adds late arrivals, partitioned state, and checkpoint recovery. Batch processing has failure and resource costs too. State whether a result is provisional: later input may change a total. A simple scheduled batch is appropriate when hours of delay are acceptable. A low-latency requirement may justify streaming, provided its recovery and state rules are designed as carefully as the normal path. || Bounded daily batch / Continuous event stream / Late events may revise output / Progress and recovery need explicit state

An ETL pipeline extracts data, transforms it, then loads the target. ELT loads first and transforms in the target environment. The difference changes where raw data exists and where computation occurs. ¶ Transforming early can minimise sensitive fields before they reach analytics. Loading raw data can support later transformations but adds storage and governance work. In either arrangement, version transformations, validate input and output, and keep ownership and lineage. Neither acronym establishes data quality or repeatability. Choose where each step belongs by its privacy, compute, and query needs rather than treating either sequence as inherently superior. || Extract source / Transform before load: ETL / Load before transform: ELT / Version and govern both paths

A source row changes, and a supported Change Data Capture mechanism records that committed change for downstream consumers. CDC can keep search or analytics current without repeatedly scanning the whole source. ¶ An initial snapshot and later change stream need a consistent handoff position. Otherwise, changes made during copying can be missed or applied wrongly. Preserve source identifiers, positions, and schema information, handle repetition, and monitor lag. A destination may expose a different or delayed view of the source's transactions. CDC transports changes; the destination still needs a clear rule for applying them and serving consistent results. || Source snapshot position recorded / Committed changes stream / Destination applies stable versions / Handoff prevents gaps and duplicate effects

A data lake can keep flexible datasets, often in object storage, while a warehouse provides curated analytical structures and serving facilities. Actual product boundaries overlap, so compare their supported operations. ¶ Raw inputs can make replay possible but also accumulate poorly understood and sensitive data. Identify immutable source versions, curated outputs, catalog information, owners, and access policies. Choose file formats and partitions for the scans and recovery work that need to run. Without schema, lineage, and lifecycle management, storing more bytes can make the data harder to trust. Retaining raw data needs a purpose, not just available space. || Raw versioned dataset / Validated curated transformation / Analytical serving layout / Catalog and retention cover every stage

An event occurred at noon but reaches a processor three minutes later. Its event time describes the source occurrence; processing time describes when the system handles it. A time-windowed result needs a rule for this late event. ¶ Stream processors maintain keyed state and progress. A watermark estimates how far event-time processing has advanced under lateness assumptions; it is not proof that no earlier event can arrive. Define accepted lateness, corrections, and when a result is final. Checkpoint and external-effect boundaries also matter. Consumers need to know whether a count can change after its first publication. || Event occurs at 10:00 / Processor receives at 10:03 / Window policy handles late data / Checkpoint restores processing state

An extracted amount is disputed. To investigate it, we need the exact file version, page or region, extractor version, parameters, and any review decision that produced it. Provenance records origins; lineage connects artifacts through transformations. ¶ Store stable references that can actually be checked. An explanatory label alone is weak if the underlying evidence cannot be located. These records support correction and reproducibility without proving every extraction right. They also have their own retention needs: copying raw personal data into a permanent audit record can defeat deletion of the original. Keep useful evidence with an explicit lifecycle. || File F version 2 / Extractor M version 5 / Field derived from page 3 / Human review and downstream output linked

A record contains both amount and currency, so it passes a structural schema check. If the amount is interpreted in the wrong units, the record is still wrong. Quality checks need semantic rules as well as field presence. ¶ Check identity, ranges, relationships, completeness, and source versions. Quarantine invalid inputs with actionable reasons and enough evidence for repair. Replaying corrected transformations must not repeat external side effects or create duplicate records. Compare source and destination business invariants alongside counts and versions. An empty error queue establishes little if a processor consistently accepts and publishes the wrong meaning. || Validate input schema / Check semantic invariant / Quarantine invalid record / Replay repaired version without duplicate effect

Deleting a PDF leaves its extracted text, thumbnail, and search entry behind. A retention policy on original bytes alone has not completed the document's lifecycle. ¶ Track every relevant copy, including logs and recoverable snapshots. A durable deletion workflow can record progress and retry unfinished steps. Minimal deletion markers may be needed to stop old workers or replay from recreating removed results; minimise the personal information retained in them. Maintain indexes that can find a user's objects efficiently. Test restoring older state against the deletion history, so recovery does not silently republish content that should remain removed. || Deletion intent recorded / Raw and derived artifacts removed / Index and caches updated / Recovery reapplies deletion decisions

Follow one document from upload to extraction, review, and search. Every step takes an identified input, applies a transformation, and produces state used by the next step. The complete design is this connected set of operations. ¶ For each connection, define the source owner, version, order, retry identity, validation, and recovery. Keep inputs recoverable where rebuilding is required, within the retention policy. Observe progress across stages rather than only queue throughput. A fast broker cannot repair missing provenance or an index serving the wrong tenant's documents. End-to-end correctness depends on how these contracts work together. || Authoritative versioned input / Validated deterministic transformations / Durable processing and effect identities / Reconciled searchable authorized output''')
